{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 32, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III was a count of Tripoli who ruled from 1152 until 1158. He was a member of the Norman family and was the son of Roger II of Sicily. Raymond III was known for his military campaigns against the Zengid dynasty which controlled much of the Middle East.\n\n* **Early Life and Reign**\nRaymond III was born in 1120 in Sicily. He was the son of Roger II and his wife Adelaide of Flanders. After his father's death in 1154 Raymond III succeeded him as the count of Tripoli. He was a just ruler and his reign was marked by peace and prosperity. Raymond III also expanded the county's borders through military campaigns. He conquered the city of Sidon from the Zengid dynasty in 1152.\n\n* **Military Campaigns and Conflict**\nRaymond III was a skilled military leader and his campaigns against the Zengid dynasty were highly successful. He also clashed with the Byzantine Empire which controlled Acre and other cities in the region. In 1157 Raymond III formed an alliance with the Crusaders who were fighting in the Third Crusade. He provided them with troops and supplies which helped them to defeat the Zengid dynasty in 1157. However his military campaigns also took a toll on his finances and he had to impose heavy taxes on his subjects to fund his wars.\n\n* **Death and Succession**\nRaymond III died suddenly in 1158 while on a military campaign against the Zengid dynasty. He was succeeded by his cousin Bohemond III who had recently returned to Tripoli after fighting in the Third Crusade. Bohemond III was only 23 years old at the time and his reign was marked by conflict and power struggles with the Byzantine Empire and the Muslim forces in the region.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0013606823049485683}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0001284993631998077}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -1.98013